MAJOR PIVOT CANDIDATE — browser as the compositor (Peter 2026-08-10, 'how good are browser terminals? maybe tmux or iframes'). Inverts candidate C: not browser-in-deck but DECK-IN-BROWSER. Assessment: browser terminals are MATURE — xterm.js powers VS Code / Codespaces / Cloud Shell (WebGL, truecolor, full escapes, mouse). ttyd/gotty/wetty expose a shell or 'tmux attach' over websocket to xterm.js; runs tmux fine (tmux doesn't care its client is xterm.js). TWO very different options: (1) tmux in ONE browser terminal (ttyd + tmux attach) = deck as-is, just reframed in a tab — ALMOST NO GAIN, still one fullscreen jumping surface. (2) TERMS AS IFRAMES / xterm.js instances laid out with CSS GRID — THE real idea: the web page IS the deck; each term is a cell; a QR picture / web view / chart is JUST ANOTHER CELL in the same grid. Dissolves nearly every frustration we forced tmux to do: NO JUMP (CSS scroll/pan/animate a wide grid — the thing tmux fundamentally can't; edge-scroll = trivial overflow; attention-grow = CSS transition), BROWSER+TERMS COEXIST by construction, identity becomes DOM (data-strand can't be clobbered like _NET_WM_NAME). COST: you build the compositor (websockets, session lifecycle, reconnection); copy/paste + browser keybinding conflicts (Ctrl-W/T/N stolen); conductor model changes from drives-tmux-CLI to drives-DOM; arguably a DIFFERENT PRODUCT. tmux may still run INSIDE each iframe for persistence/scrollback, or skip it (iframe = direct ttyd→aicli). This is the natural home for EVERY layout idea spooled (standard layout, attention-reflow, ribbon, pan, stable addressing). Biggest/most promising direction the strand has surfaced — needs a proper design pass + a spike (ttyd + 2 iframes + CSS grid + one aicli keeper).
